# Every claim filed as a point, beside who it says owns the edge. One row per point claim,
# `violates` where the answer is that the bound is where the measurements fell.
#
# The schema's two sentences, side by side
#   The rule is the schema's own two sentences put beside each other.
#   `boundOrigin absent/reason = none` is documented as: "nothing sets it. The bound is where the
#   measurements fell, not where a rule put them, and there is no lever to look for." A point
#   value has no range, so nothing fell anywhere. The sentence describes a spread the claim does
#   not have, and it is the answer a receiver would act on (go measure again and the bound will
#   move) against a figure filed as though measuring again would change nothing.
#
# The schema already names the right answer
#   It is in the entry directly below. `notApplicable` is "the quantity has no author to ask
#   about: an observed count, a derived residual". An exact observation is precisely that.
#   `derived` is the other honest answer, and it is what the great majority of point claims here
#   give: the figure is stated in a sibling element (`Nameplate/amountOrigin`,
#   `LumpyQuantum/origin`, and on an elimination `asrt:against` plus `asrt:between`), which is
#   what licenses filing it without a width. So this rule does not forbid an exact claim. It asks
#   that the exactness have an author, and the enum has a member for every honest one.
#
# The corpus contains its own control
#   Among the `pm:jagged/pm:draw` claims, the points all answer `none`; the one with a spread
#   answers `derived`, under a comment that reads "a point draw converts into a range for the
#   same reason the nameplate does: the cards drawn are exact, the hours they were drawn for are
#   not." In the single place a draw is not a point, the author writes down why the others are.
#   The rule imposes no reading; it recites one the documents already make somewhere else. Look
#   for that shape before acting on any first run: the sibling that breaks the pattern is the
#   author's own control, and it is worth more than the count of the ones that fit.
#
# The third corner of a square
#   The pair is why it is visible at all. Two rules already cross a claim's width against
#   `narrowsWhen`: a point must file `notApplicable`, a range must not. This crosses the same
#   width against `boundOrigin`, the other half of the required pair. Neither column alone can
#   see it: `none` is one of the two commonest boundOrigin answers and unremarkable on its own,
#   and the width is unremarkable on its own.
#
# What would refute it
#   A quantity that is genuinely exact, genuinely unowned, and genuinely not an observed count.
#   The schema's own gloss on `none` argues no such thing exists, since an unowned bound is by
#   construction one that measurement placed.
-- pm:Claim/pm:boundOrigin with pm:absent/reason = none on a claim where low = high.
SELECT r.rule, p.filing, p.layer, p.violates, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(checks/roster.sqlc)
) r
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT c.filing, c.layer,
           c.origin_absent IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'none' AS violates,
           format('%s claim %s: %s %s exactly, and nothing is said to set it',
                  c.owns, c.seq, c.mode, c.unit) AS detail
    FROM (
        :compose(epistemics/point_claims.sqlc)
    ) c
) p ON true
WHERE r.slug = 'bound_fell_with_no_range'
